                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Typical candidates are patients with cervical disc herniation or foraminal stenosis causing radiculopathy who have persistent symptoms despite conservative treatment, with pathology localized to a single or limited number of levels and without significant instability or deformity."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "When is posterior endoscopic cervical foraminotomy preferred over anterior approaches?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Posterior endoscopic cervical foraminotomy is often preferred for lateral or foraminal stenosis in patients with preserved cervical alignment when motion preservation is desired and anterior pathology such as central disc herniation is limited."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "What are the advantages of endoscopic thoracic decompression compared with open surgery?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Endoscopic thoracic decompression minimizes soft tissue disruption, preserves paraspinal musculature and thoracic structures, reduces postoperative pain, shortens hospital stay, and avoids many thoracotomy-related complications seen with traditional open approaches."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "Is endoscopic lumbar discectomy effective for recurrent disc herniation?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Yes. Endoscopic lumbar discectomy is an effective option for recurrent disc herniation, allowing targeted removal of recurrent fragments while minimizing scar tissue disruption and preserving stabilizing structures compared with repeat open surgery."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "What is the expected recovery timeline after endoscopic cervical or thoracic surgery?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Most patients are able to ambulate on the day of surgery or the next day, return to light daily activities within several days, and resume normal function within a few weeks, depending on the level treated, underlying pathology, and overall health status."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "Are there patients who should not undergo endoscopic spine surgery?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Contraindications include marked spinal instability, significant deformity requiring realignment, spinal infection, tumors requiring en bloc resection, or situations where endoscopy cannot provide adequate decompression or stabilization."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                name:
                  "How does endoscopic spine surgery compare to fusion surgery in terms of motion preservation?",
                acceptedAnswer: {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  text:
                    "Endoscopic spine surgery focuses on removing the offending pathology while preserving native motion segments, avoiding the stiffness and adjacent segment degeneration associated with fusion procedures in appropriately selected patients."
                }
