      <section class="mb-12 space-y-4" id="patients">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">Information for Patients</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-neutral-700">
          The following questions summarize how endoscopic spine surgery is
          typically applied in clinical practice. Answers are general
          information and do not replace an in-person consultation or
          individualized medical advice.
        </p>

        <section class="space-y-3" id="faq">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-neutral-800">
            Endoscopic Spine Surgery – FAQ
          </h4>
          <div class="space-y-3 text-sm">
            <div class="rounded-lg border bg-white p-4">
              <div class="font-semibold">
                Which patients are candidates for anterior endoscopic cervical
                decompression?
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-neutral-700">
                Patients with cervical disc herniation or foraminal stenosis
                causing arm pain or radiculopathy, who have persistent symptoms
                despite conservative treatment and have pathology limited to a
                small number of levels without severe deformity or instability,
                may be considered for anterior endoscopic decompression.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-lg border bg-white p-4">
              <div class="font-semibold">
                When is posterior endoscopic cervical foraminotomy preferred
                over anterior approaches?
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-neutral-700">
                Posterior endoscopic cervical foraminotomy is often chosen for
                lateral or foraminal stenosis in patients with preserved
                cervical alignment when motion preservation is important and
                central disc compression is limited.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-lg border bg-white p-4">
              <div class="font-semibold">
                What are the advantages of endoscopic thoracic decompression
                compared with open surgery?
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-neutral-700">
                Endoscopic thoracic decompression can reduce muscle damage and
                postoperative pain, shorten hospital stay, and avoid many
                thoracotomy-related complications, while still providing
                effective decompression in properly selected cases.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-lg border bg-white p-4">
              <div class="font-semibold">
                Is endoscopic lumbar discectomy effective for recurrent disc
                herniation?
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-neutral-700">
                Yes. In many patients, endoscopic lumbar discectomy offers a
                targeted way to remove recurrent disc fragments while limiting
                scar dissection and preserving stabilizing structures, which can
                be advantageous compared with repeat open surgery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-lg border bg-white p-4">
              <div class="font-semibold">
                What is the typical recovery timeline after endoscopic cervical
                or thoracic surgery?
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-neutral-700">
                Many patients are able to walk on the same day or the next day,
                resume light daily activities within several days, and return to
                normal function within a few weeks, depending on the level
                treated, the underlying condition, and overall health.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-lg border bg-white p-4">
              <div class="font-semibold">
                Are there patients who should not undergo endoscopic spine
                surgery?
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-neutral-700">
                Patients with severe spinal instability, significant deformity
                requiring realignment, active infection, or tumors requiring
                wider resection are generally not good candidates for endoscopic
                surgery and may need more extensive procedures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="rounded-lg border bg-white p-4">
              <div class="font-semibold">
                How does endoscopic surgery compare to fusion surgery for motion
                preservation?
              </div>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-neutral-700">
                When appropriate, endoscopic surgery removes the compressive
                pathology while preserving motion segments, reducing the risk of
                stiffness and adjacent segment degeneration that can occur after
                fusion.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
